Minato (M6 c2, ADR-0053): the runtime side of compiled code decodes references. Patch 0009 decodes what the code
generator reads; a reference that compiled code hands to the runtime, or that the runtime reads from a compiled frame,
is still the low 32 bits of the object's address (heap_window.h), and C++ reads its mirror arguments as addresses.
 - the downcall macros take the registers that hold references (`refs`) and decode them (DECODE_REF_OR_NULL, in place,
   after the frame saved the arguments as they came): the allocation, field and fill-array entrypoints of
   quick_alloc_entrypoints.S and quick_field_entrypoints.S, with the class or object of each named at its use; the TLAB
   array stubs decode x0 before the slow path; the class initialization stub decodes its class;
 - art_quick_deliver_exception decodes the exception, art_quick_check_instance_of's bitstring path the object, and the
   resolution and proxy trampolines their receiver;
 - HClearException stores 64 bits: Thread::exception is a pointer, and with the address B | reference its upper half
   stayed B, so the exception stayed pending;
 - the JNI stubs of compiled native methods refresh x20 from the thread's heap window base when they return from
   native code, not from the GC-marking flag (patch 0007 changed the stubs of the assembly files and not the JNI
   compiler's);
 - the roots of a compiled frame's callee-save registers are the decode of the register's low half;
 - the intrinsics decode: every HeapOperand use, Unsafe's accessors (a null base is an absolute address),
   String.equals, getChars, System.arraycopy (the references are compared and tested as W);
 - runtime/art/codec-sites.txt lists the places; tools/art-codec-sites.py checks it.

